**Study:** [Outgassing properties of vacuum materials — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Outgassing-Properties-Chiggiato.lingua#r_18MM2A316NWRJABS8E53468PWT) and [Comparing vacuum-chamber materials and treatments — Fedchak and colleagues](../sources/Outgassing-Comparison-Fedchak.lingua#r_0CR7DXJZ2Z6J97G0NGV2RQNM8D), complete texts; [Vacuum systems — Vincent Baglin and Roberto Kersevan](../sources/Vacuum-Systems-Baglin-Kersevan.lingua#r_1K9ATEHFM7ET2H8TRHC3QNCRFN), material qualification; the shared diffusion and measurement work.

Choose an observed or explicitly hypothetical slow pressure response. Develop competing explanations involving finite surface inventory, bulk diffusion, permeation, a trapped pocket and an external leak. Derive the predicted dependence on time, temperature, thickness, exposure and gas identity under each model.

Identify quantities that are not recoverable from one pressure trace. Design a sequence of discriminating observations that changes one consequential condition at a time. Include gauge response, background, readsorption and uncertainty. Preserve the specimen's chronology so that a conditioning step is not mistaken for a repeat of the original experiment.

**Deliver:** A comparison of mechanisms with independently checked limiting calculations and a justified next observation. Reconstruct an accessible primary result or perform a documented measurement within a suitable laboratory. State any missing raw data. Revise the explanation after a result that disagrees with its prediction.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
**Continue in gas and vacuum systems:** [Review pumping, material history and the measured state](Review-pumping-material-history-and-the-measured-state.lingua#r_5QS86VWMREAY4AADFCSZEB5XKQ).

